 difficult seven months for us as medical  personnel I decided to write a list of  things that were good and bad about our  deployment and of course this was a  source of great entertainment for the  people that I deployed with because they  thought that's going to be a pretty  lopsided list but I decided to do it  anyway  it was for me at that moment something  that was needed it was it was therapy it  was my own way of starting to process  the experience it ended up taking on  oddly the form of a poem of sorts and I  sent it via email to my husband I had  been sending him updates through email  the entire time and he had been  forwarding those things on to a group of  about 25 family and friends the poem  mm-hmm through the power of our beloved  Internet was forwarded literally around  the world  so by the time I returned to Florida the  list which is what I called it had made  its way to literally around the world  and to many different parts of life both  military and civilian leading to  multiple requests for permission to  reprint it as well as requests for  interviews at first especially upon  returning home I found the attention to  be pretty overwhelming and even  embarrassing but then the email started  coming in and they were emails from  Vietnam and Korea and World War two vets  Marines and Navy corpsman and nurses and  they told me that the poem made them cry  and then my attitude changed and I was  humbled one of those Vietnam Marines a  retired lieutenant colonel and Pulitzer  Prize nominated author otto lyric  contacted me and he convinced me that  the experiences in the poem needed to be  expanded in written form for some reason  still unclear on that I decided to go  ahead and start writing it's sort of a  big time and Otto agreed to help me  I never at that point had any intention  or even vague thought of publishing this  I wrote it as therapy and interestingly  writing of traumatic experiences has now  become a very established and accepted  form of therapy in order to treat PTSD  post-traumatic stress disorder at the  time I just wasn't really aware that  that's what I was doing so I wrote these  stories not only to process my own  feelings but also as a record for my  children I returned home just before  their second birthday and they had  changed from babies to toddlers of  course with no memory whatsoever of mom  in the desert so I wanted them to have  that one year  250 pages later Auto passed me on to his  literary agent also a former Marine who  convinced me to move forward with  submission to publishing houses today I  feel very fortunate to be working with  little brown and company and I'm very  excited at last for the publication of  rule number 2 in October in thinking  about the way to speak to you today I  decided that it was pretty important  actually to share the poem since it did  start at all and it is the foundation on  which the book was built while I read it  I'll have some pictures going here  I hope sunset over the desert almost  always orange sunrise over the desert  almost always red the childlike  excitement of having fresh fruit at  dinner after going weeks without it I'm  sorry this starts with things that were  good I've know this so well that I  almost forget things that we're good  these are the things that were good  being allowed to be the kind of  clinician I know I can be and want to be  with no limits placed and no doubts  expressed but most of all the United  States Marines are patients walking  every day and having literally every  single person who passed say hoorah  man having them tell us one after  another through blinding pain or  morphine induced euphoria when can I get  out of here I just want to get back to  my unit meeting a young sergeant who  lost his eye in an explosion he asked  his surgeon if he could open the other  one and when he did he sat up and looked  at the young Marines from his fire team  who were being treated for superficial  shrapnel wounds he smiled laid back down  and said I only have one good eye doc  but I can see that my Marines are ok and  of course meeting the one I will never  forget the one who threw himself on a  grenade to save the men at his side who  will likely be the first Medal of Honor  recipient in over 11 years  my friends some of them are lifelong in  a way that is indescribable my patients  some of them had courage unlike anything  I had ever experienced before my  comrades at Alfa surgical company some  of the things witnessed will traumatize  them forever but they provided  outstanding care to those Marines day in  and day out sometimes for days at a time  with no break for seven endless months  and finally above all else holding the  hand of that dying marine things that  were not good  terrifying camel spiders poisonous  scorpions flapping bats in the darkness  territorial wild dogs flies that  insisted on landing on our faces  giant looming mosquitoes invisible sand  flies that carry leishmaniasis 132  degrees wearing long sleeves full pants  and combat boots in 132 degrees random  and totally predictable power outages  that led to sweating throughout the  night sweating in places I didn't know I  could sweat like wrists and ears the  roar of helicopters overhead the  resounding thud of exploding artillery  in the distance the popping of gunfire  not knowing if any of the above sounds  is a good thing or a bad thing the siren  and the inevitable big voice telling us  to take cover not knowing if that siren  was on someone's DVD or if the big voice  would soon follow the cracking sound of  giant artillery rounds splitting open  against rock and dirt the rumble of the  ground the shattering of the windows  hiding under flak jackets and Kevlar  helmets away from the broken windows  waiting to be told that we could come to  the hospital to treat those that were  not so lucky watching the black  helicopter with the big Red Cross  landing at our pad worse watching  gray marine helicopters full of patients  landing at our pad because we usually  did not realize they were coming  assuring a sobbing Marine colonel away  from the trauma Bay where several of his  Marines bled and cried out in pain  inside meeting a 21 year old corporal  with three purple hearts and listening  to him weep because he felt ashamed of  being afraid to go back telling a room  of stunned Marines and blood-soaked  uniforms that their comrade that they  had tried to save had just died of his  wounds trying as if in total futility to  do anything I could to ease the trauma  of group after group that suffered loss  after loss grief after inconsolable  grief washing blood off the boots of one  of our young nurses while she told me  about the one who bled out in the trauma  Bay and then the one she had to tell  when he pleaded for the truth that his  best friend didn't make it  listening to another of our year nurses  tell of the Marine who came in talking  telling her his name about how she  pleaded with him not to give up told him  she was there for him about how she  could see his eyes go dull when he  couldn't fight any longer and finally  above all else holding the hand of that  dying marine the dying marine to whom I  refer in the poem is corporal Jason  Dunham in January of this year I was  invited by his father and mother to  attend the White House ceremony where  President Bush posthumously awarded the  Congressional Medal of Honor  to corporal Dunham Jason Dunham in  jumping on a grenade gave his life to  save the people on his fire team the  Marines and sailors with whom I had the  privilege to serve in Iraq had that same  spirit the raw courage and undying  commitment to one another  that spirit was the inspiration for rule  number two  it's the reason I wrote it it's the  reason I went forward to try to have it  published and it's the reason I stand  before you today it's a common theme  throughout the book these stories  provide a unique window for people who  have not been in Iraq to understand just  a little bit about the type of people  that are fighting for us out there it is  also a common theme in the two short  chapters which I've chosen to read for  you from the book today  the story is alluded to in the poem it's  called one good eye an urgent call  crackled through the speaker on my  handheld radio triage request for combat  stress  this meant possible cut side  psychological intervention for  casualties when I arrived I found our  sick call very busy with less seriously  wounded patients filling exam rooms and  spilling into the passageway in the  center of the activity bill one of our  physician assistants looked calm let's  get these guys cleaned up he told one of  the corpsman who guided three Marines  and blood-stained uniforms to a cleared  area in the passageway and seated them  on cots each had been hit with shrapnel  in his arms legs or face but all three  were walking and talking bill  acknowledged me with a smile as he  hurried back to an exam room the power  was out across the base and the hospital  felt like a sauna  except of course the o.r the ward and  the lab where the backup generators ran  tiny window air-conditioning units  Bill's blouse was off and sweat darkened  his green t-shirt and glistened off the  9-millimeter pistol hanging over his  shoulders and a leather holster ie D I  asked yep just those three in there no  their sergeants in here he motioned  toward the open door his hands full of  supplies he lowered his voice and leaned  towards me he lost his eye in the  explosion the lid is intact but the eye  itself is gone except for the single  blood black rod which maybe the optic  nerve I followed him into the exam room  where a stocky marine with dark hair lay  on the makeshift table his slightly  deformed left eyelid and superficial  lacerations on his cheek and chin would  never have suggested that the eye  underneath was missing he moaned as bill  opened with his lid with gloved hands  the okay sergeant bill asked him I'm  just taking the look here real quick  sure the patient said quietly biting his  lip I drew in my breath when I saw what  was under his eyelid a thin dark stem of  tissue protruded from the gaping empty  socket  would be sent via urgent medevac to  Baghdad to see an ophthalmologist if it  involved life limb or eyesight the helo  was here in 30 minutes amazing huh bill  whispered I nodded as we looked at each  other  just then the Marine grabbed Bill's  wrist hey Doc he asked what's up  sergeant my eyes not gonna make it is it  I'm not gonna lie to you it's not good  bill answered carefully my mom's gonna  kill me the patient joked with a wry  grin bill and I smiled - hey doc yes is  my other eye okay it looks that way to  me can I open it sure bill said let me  just get something to patch the injured  one first though okay he motioned for  one of the corpsman who moved in to  place gauze and medical tape over the  mutilated eye the Marine asked for help  to sit up opened his intact eye and  scanned the passageway he recognized the  members of his fireteam who by now had  their shirts off and were having their  wounds dressed he silently counted them  he sighed with a smile and gingerly  lowered himself back to the cot thanks  doc he said closing his eye I only have  one good eye but I can see that my  Marines are okay  and the second chapter I've chosen is  called woman's best friend sometimes  when a black hawk with its bright Red  Cross landed on our pad and swirl of  dust the patient brought in on the  gurney was not a Marine sailor soldier  or even an enemy prisoner of war  sometimes the patient was a dog the  Marines had a large Corps of working  dogs beautiful sinewy shepherds who  aided the mission in Iraq through the  detection of explosives weapons and bad  guys each dog had a Marine handler and  hundreds of these duo's were spread  throughout the country the good news was  the dogs were treated like royalty with  guaranteed air-conditioned spaces and  great food the bad news was sometimes  they needed a doctor our veterinarian  was Army Major Tim lunin an extroverted  man with a warm engaging grin who had  once been a paratrooper sharing  resources with our operating room an  intensive care unit allowed Tim to build  a complete animal surgical suite in the  basement of the hospital he knew of my  fondness for dogs so he sent word to me  whenever he had a quote lover a working  dog that adored being adored not all of  them fit this category by the way  whenever a lover arrived at Tim's shop I  found a way to spend some time in the  basement talking to the gorgeous animal  while I stroked his head and kissed the  bridge of his long nose in Iraq I  developed a new appreciation for pet  therapy I believe Tim knew how happy I  was to have this unique opportunity to  escape the desert for a few magical  moments with his canine charges but I do  not believe I ever told him how much I  appreciated his role in saving one of my  patients lives sergeant sergeant  Hutchison and I should say all the  patients names and identities are  changed in this book  sergeant Hutchison a petite petite  brunette with Frankel's freckles  sprinkled across her nose was a  helicopter flight equipment specialist  like many marine pilots and aircraft  maintenance personnel she found herself  in Iraq filling an unfamiliar role in  her  as a member of an auxilary Support  Battalion grueling to convoy duty took  its toll on everyone in her company but  sergeant Hutchison had two other  problems in addition to fatigue and  vulnerability she had a history of  clinical depression and she was the only  woman in her company she came to me  early in the deployment feeling  lethargic weepy hopeless and fleetingly  suicidal the sergeant and I had a  heart-to-heart she did not want to be  sent home through tears she begged to  stay although she worried about how she  would succeed she said she wanted to  finish the deployment to prove to both  her unit and herself that she could I  believed her Jason and I worked together  combining antidepressant medication and  intensive therapy to teach her to block  her self-defeating thought processes for  a while she made progress and slowly  experienced more energy and self-worth  halfway through the summer the sergeant  experienced the loss of one of her  friends in an IUD explosion this caused  a downward spiral in her functioning  that I feared pointed to her medevac she  told me her future was hopeless she  spoke of falling asleep and never  awakening she and I worked long into the  evening that day and I could almost hear  her fingernails digging in as she  struggled to maintain her grip on mental  health she held on the operational tempo  of her unit increased and she was unable  to see me for almost two months  and then one day four weeks before we  returned home sergeant Hutchison strode  into our office a different woman her  hair was pulled back her cheeks were  rosy and I think I saw a trace of lip  gloss  she smiled brightly I had never seen her  smile before our unit has adopted this  puppy she started as she sat down I had  heard about several groups of Marines  finding the orphaned puppies of wild  dogs on base and making them unofficial  mascots feeding them with shipments of  puppy chow sent from home everybody  liked him but he always came to me he  sort of became mine anyway I heard some  people were sending their puppies home  my heart sank a little immediately  worried that my sensitive patient would  be devastated if her plan did not  succeed so I came to talk to major lunin  I smiled proud of her initiative  he's mice isn't he Oh ma'am he's so nice  she gushed he took a look at Koko that's  what I'm calling him because he looks  like the color of chocolate anyway he  gave him all his shots and did the  operation to neuter him and he did the  worm medication to major Lunan says he's  in great health I had to admit I was