es  back and and has been shot and needs  surgery and Hawkeye operates on him and  he loses him on the table  and he is distraught and Henry who is my  favorite character in all of mash comes  in and he says you know I don't know  much I only know what they taught me in  commanding officer school and they  taught us that there are two rules of  war rule number one is that young men  die and rule number two is the doctors  can't change rule number one mm-hmm  other questions  kind of big spirit what kinds of  experiences emotions did you go through  it's it's not too personal it's  something that now a couple years later  I feel like I've processed but you're  looking at part of the therapy process  connection after an experience like this  connection back with a world that's not  at war is intensely difficult it is it's  not as difficult as combat but it is  uniquely almost as difficult I think and  I think a lot of people experience this  that things out there while scary and  sometimes horrific and sometimes very  sad they had a very defined order and a  very set plan and we had blinders on and  it was it was quite easy to to not have  to deal with most of our grief loss fear  any of it for that matter because we had  a job to do and we were working seven  days a week 12 14 20 hours a day quite  frankly we were too tired so none of it  got dealt with and I know I'm just only  speaking for myself here but I think  that this is a theme I'm seeing with my  patients now as well that I think while  we're out there we're not paying  attention to anything that needs to be  processed and how could we we wouldn't  be able to function so we come home and  suddenly that's when all of it  surfaces so not only is there this  strange juxtaposition of a world at  peace in this world that we were just in  which is at war there's also a real  difficulty in my case at least with  connecting with people who didn't  understand it's a the movie Band of  Brothers is called that for a reason  there's this this feeling that it's very  very difficult to engage the people who  who weren't there because you walk a  fine line of disclosing too much that  that maybe a person doesn't want to hear  all this  or that maybe there's no way they could  ever understand so why even try why even  start to go there so it took many many  months of struggle with my husband and  and my children for me to try to find  the place where I could start to explain  and I had it easy my husband's a marine  I think of these spouses who have no  knowledge of the military it's a very  it's a very different difficult clash of  worlds I also had a really difficult  time going back to clinical practice  seeing people who were not combat vets  which I was at Naval Hospital  Jacksonville got pulled out of there by  myself to deploy with the San Diego unit  so got returned to Jacksonville a  hospital where no one had deployed to  Iraq and all my patients were not  dealing with any of this so it was it's  very difficult for me to find empathy  for what are very real problems there's  nothing nothing nothing to say that  their problems and the things that we're  upsetting them were not real but I had a  I I couldn't I couldn't sort of get out  of my you know you call that a problem  sensation it took me a long time to get  back to realizing that everyone's  problems are on a continuum and just  because someone isn't dying in your arms  doesn't mean it's not painful so it was  a long haul and writing the book  actually helped a tremendous amount yes  ma'am  thank you dogs do you have a dog I  always did grew up with one and had one  up until the point when I married my  husband who is not a dog person I would  have one in a heartbeat but he would not  so but I loved him yeah I think you're  right on as far as their even if you are  a dog person the therapeutic value of  having dogs they've certainly seen that  to be the case in with aging elderly  nursing homes now they have red pen sand  dogs and and for all kinds of handicaps  and I live on a farm and I have two dogs  and  I don't know what I I don't know how I  ever existed without them they're  awesome yeah it's a there they're  wonderful and such a strange yeah a  strange sort of unexpected gift the time  we had with those working dogs and it's  a woman I mean that that just turned her  around it clinically it did is a  wonderful story thank you thank you I  actually this is the Army Major the  veterinarian I used his real name  patients names are all changed but I  used his real name and it'd be  interesting to see if I couldn't find  him before I wrote the book but it'll be  interesting to see if he gets in touch  and says I didn't know that such a great  guy yes sir may be a little unfair  because you have a this current  experience but PTSD I think there's that  that it's kind of a new nomenclature  kind of a thing for a lot of us is the  experience that that results in that  different in your this current conflict  is it really different from kinds of  things that happen to now I'm at Korea  PTSD can be from any kind of trauma they  the key diagnostic factors mean that the  purse that include that the person has  to experience helplessness hopelessness  terror or somehow another sort of beyond  the realm of human experience feeling so  we see PTSD and rape survivors assault  survivors survivors of national natural  disasters combat it kind of has its own  slant you can imagine but no I I think  each conflict of course has its special  flavor sort of things that that people  say that they deal with I think the use  of children as combatants in this  conflict have been difficult for our  guys not knowing whether a child is  armed or not whether a child is going to  throw a grenade whether a child is going  to blow a whistle that leads somebody  else to pop it you know blow a grenade  or ie D some of those things are a  little bit unique possibly although I  understand it also happened in 